From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 00:00:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] input: kpd_customkey: run the key handler in a thread

key_irq_handler() is registered with devm_request_irq(), so it runs in hard
IRQ context - and then calls two things that may sleep:

	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&data->sleep_work);
	...
	mutex_lock(&data->lock);

cancel_delayed_work_sync() waits for a running work item to finish, and
mutex_lock() blocks on contention. Neither is allowed with interrupts off.
It survives in practice only because the work is usually not pending and
the mutex is usually uncontended, which makes it the kind of bug that bites
under load rather than on the bench; CONFIG_DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP reports it as
"BUG: sleeping function called from invalid context".

Ask for a threaded handler instead. Passing NULL as the primary handler
means every edge is taken straight to the IRQ thread, where sleeping is
legal, so both calls become correct as written and neither has to be
restructured. IRQF_ONESHOT is required for a threaded IRQ with no primary
handler, and is wanted here anyway: it keeps the line masked until the
thread has finished, which debounces a switch that is read in that thread.

No functional change beyond the context the handler runs in.

Not-signed-off: add your own Signed-off-by before sending this to FuriLabs.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [ODM driver, exists only in the vendor tree]
---
--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/kpd_customkey.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/kpd_customkey.c
@@ -253,8 +253,10 @@
     }
 
     for (i = 0; i < data->key_count; i++) {
-        ret = devm_request_irq(&pdev->dev, data->keys[i].irq, key_irq_handler,
-                              IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING | IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING,
+        ret = devm_request_threaded_irq(&pdev->dev, data->keys[i].irq,
+                              NULL, key_irq_handler,
+                              IRQF_TRIGGER_RISING | IRQF_TRIGGER_FALLING |
+                              IRQF_ONESHOT,
                               data->keys[i].name, &data->keys[i]);
         if (ret) {
             dev_err(&pdev->dev, "Failed to request IRQ %d for key %s\n", 
